# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""Benevolence the Root — English reading."""

TITLE = 'Chapter One — Benevolence the Root'
HEAD = '''Ten passages. The opening chapter, and the least like a military book of any chapter in the seven: it hardly says how to fight at all, only when one may fight and what one may not do while fighting. Two of its sentences are the best known in the work — “if by war you put an end to war, then even war is permissible,” and “though a state be large, to love war is surely to perish; though the realm be at peace, to forget war is surely to be in danger.” The second half sets out a procedure for going to war: the offending state is proclaimed to all the feudal lords, then to August Heaven and the Lord on High, to the sun, moon and stars, to the Earth Queen and the spirits of the four seas, the rivers and the hills, and last to the former kings — only then does the Prime Minister levy troops. War, in this book, is first of all a rite. Then comes a field discipline: do not wreck the earthworks, do not burn the houses, do not fell the trees, send the old and the young home unharmed, and <i>if the enemy is wounded, give him medicine and send him back</i>. The closing passage, the nine prohibitions, is almost word for word the “nine punitive expeditions” of the <i>Rites of Zhou</i> — the hardest single piece of evidence that this book belongs to the literature of military <i>rites</i>. The Han imperial bibliography enters the <i>Methods of the Sima</i> in 155 chapters not among the military writers but under the classics, in the section on rites.'''

PARA = {
 'p1-1': 'In antiquity benevolence was taken as the root, and to govern by rightness was called uprightness. When uprightness fails to attain its object, there is expedience. Expedience issues from war; it does not issue from ordinary men. Therefore if by killing men you bring men peace, killing is permissible; if by attacking a state you love its people, attacking is permissible; if by war you put an end to war, then even war is permissible. And so benevolence is met with affection, rightness with delight, wisdom with reliance, courage with men who will give their bodies, good faith with trust. Within, you win their love, and so can hold what you have; without, you win awe, and so can fight. (The base text read “to govern by rightness, and governing by it is uprightness,” and “expedience issues from war, not from benevolence.” Both witnesses read as translated; the second of these makes no sense as it stands — expedience is exactly what benevolence does not yield — and both have been emended. See the colophon. For “courage with men who will give their bodies” the Siku text has “courage is met with squareness.” A book called <i>Benevolence the Root</i> opens by licensing killing and invasion, with the conditions written into the same sentence.)',
 'p1-2': 'The way of war: do not go against the season, and do not fall upon a people when they are sick — this is how you love your own people. Do not strike a state in mourning, do not take advantage of a famine — this is how you love the other people. Do not raise an army in deep winter or high summer — this is how you love both peoples at once. And so, though a state be large, to love war is surely to perish; though the realm be at peace, to forget war is surely to be in danger. (The last two clauses are the most quoted sentence in the book, and the frame of the whole of it: loving war and forgetting war are two diseases, and this book stands on neither side. The Siku text reads “love <i>their</i> people” in the third clause.)',
 'p1-3': 'When the realm has been pacified, the Son of Heaven has the victory music played, and holds the spring hunt and the autumn hunt; the feudal lords muster their ranks in spring and drill their troops in autumn. All of this is so that war shall not be forgotten. (The two hunts are the seasonal chases of the <i>Rites of Zhou</i>, and are also exercises. The Sibu congkan text reads “the realm has the victory music played.” The previous passage says that loving war destroys; this one gives the prescription for forgetting war — put the drill into the calendar.)',
 'p1-4': 'In antiquity: they did not pursue a routed enemy more than a hundred paces, and did not follow a withdrawing one more than three days’ march, and by this they made their observance of the rites plain; they did not press the helpless to the wall, and they pitied the wounded and the sick, and by this they made their rightness plain; they waited until the other side had formed its ranks and then beat the drum, and by this they made their good faith plain; they contended for right and not for gain, and by this they made their rightness plain; they were able to let the submissive go, and by this they made their courage plain; they knew how a thing ends as well as how it begins, and by this they made their wisdom plain. These six virtues, taught together in their seasons, are the way to give a people its bonds. Such was the government of antiquity. (Here is the gravest variant in the book. The six virtues ought to be rites, benevolence, good faith, rightness, courage and wisdom; but the base text and the Sibu congkan facsimile both say “made their <i>rightness</i> plain” twice, so that only five can be counted. The Siku text reads “made their <i>benevolence</i> plain” in the first of the two, which gives the full six and answers the chapter’s own title. This page emends only where both witnesses agree against the base text; here there is only one, so the base reading stands and the case is recorded. It is the one place where I believe the base text is wrong and have not changed it.)',
 'p1-5': 'The government of the former kings followed the way of heaven, disposed what was fitting to the earth, and gave office to the virtuous among the people; and so names were rectified and things put in order, states were founded and duties distinguished, and revenue was apportioned by rank. The feudal lords were glad and came in, those beyond the seas submitted, lawsuits died away and weapons lay down. Such is government by the virtue of a sage. (“Gave office to” is a verb here. The Siku text reads “the <i>perfection</i> of sagely virtue.”)',
 'p1-6': 'Next after these, worthy kings made rites, music, laws and measures; they instituted the five punishments and raised armour and arms, in order to punish the unrighteous. They toured the four quarters, assembled the feudal lords, and inquired where usage was not uniform. If any had failed in his charge, disordered the constant norms, turned his back on virtue and gone against the seasons of heaven, and endangered a lord who had done good service, then it was proclaimed to all the feudal lords and his guilt made manifest. Then proclamation was made to August Heaven and the Lord on High, to sun, moon and stars; prayer was offered to the Earth Queen, to the spirits of the four seas, to the mountains, rivers and burial mounds; and at last report was made to the former kings. Only after that did the Prime Minister levy troops from the feudal lords, saying: “Such a state has acted without the Way, and is to be punished. On such a day of such a month of such a year the army will reach that state and assemble for the Son of Heaven to carry out the punishment.” (For “toured the four quarters” the Siku text reads “toured and surveyed the regions,” which runs better; the base reading is kept. For “proclaimed to all” both witnesses have the graph that unambiguously means <i>to all</i>; the base text’s graph is the ordinary loan for it and is kept. The Sibu congkan text has a different graph for the burial mounds. The whole passage is a procedure — the lords first, then heaven and earth, then the ancestors, and only then the levy. It is what most sharply separates this book from the other six.)',
 'p1-7': 'The Prime Minister and the hundred officers published the order to the army, saying: “When you enter the territory of the guilty, do no violence to the spirits of the place; do not hunt; do not destroy earthworks; do not burn houses; do not fell trees; do not take livestock, grain or implements. When you meet the old and the young, escort them home and do them no harm. Even if you meet a man of fighting age, do not engage him unless he resists you. If an enemy has been wounded, give him medicine and send him home.” (“Earthworks” are dykes and channels. The Siku text has a different negative in “escort them home unharmed.” This is the earliest field discipline surviving in Chinese, seven prohibitions running one after another; and it ends by ordering medical care for the enemy’s wounded and their repatriation — more than two thousand years before the first Geneva Convention of 1864.)',
 'p1-8': 'When the guilty had been punished, the king and the feudal lords set that state in order, raised up its worthy men, established its clear-sighted men, and restored its proper offices. (Restoring the offices, not annexing the state, is the point. Conquest is not the object — this sentence is where the field discipline above comes to rest.)',
 'p1-9': 'There are six means by which a king or a hegemon governs the feudal lords: he defines their borders by territory; he keeps them level by ordinance; he draws them close by rites and good faith; he wins them over by talent and strength; he holds them together by men who can plan; he makes them submit by armour and arms. He unites them by sharing their troubles and their gains; he keeps them in concord by having the small serve the great. (Of the six, the first five require no soldiers; armour and arms come last.)',
 'p1-10': 'There are nine prohibitions proclaimed when the lords are assembled: him who oppresses the weak and encroaches on the few, reduce; him who injures the worthy and harms the people, punish by arms; him who is cruel within and overbearing without, depose; him whose fields lie waste and whose people scatter, pare down his lands; him who relies on his fastnesses and will not submit, invade; him who murders his kin, bring to formal justice; him who drives out or kills his ruler, destroy; him who breaks the ordinances and slights the government, cut off from his neighbours; him who is licentious within and without and behaves as the beasts do, wipe out. (These nine are almost word for word the “nine punitive expeditions” of the <i>Rites of Zhou</i>, in the office of the Grand Minister of War. The <i>Methods of the Sima</i> is a book of military <i>rites</i>, and this is the hardest internal evidence for it.)',
}
